Negative control for filter-renderer.test.ts.

Stops the range control merging its write onto the column's current value, so
setting one end silently drops the other — the renderer deciding what the column
meant, which is what scoping the column rather than a leaf costs if the write is
not a merge. The assertion that reads the whole column after both ends are set
goes red; one that sets a single end, or reads the declaration rather than a
write, stays green.

diff --git a/packages/client-vue/src/components/form/renderers/FilterRangeRenderer.vue b/packages/client-vue/src/components/form/renderers/FilterRangeRenderer.vue
index 41e1f4b25..2a7c9cc9a 100644
--- a/packages/client-vue/src/components/form/renderers/FilterRangeRenderer.vue
+++ b/packages/client-vue/src/components/form/renderers/FilterRangeRenderer.vue
@@ -103,7 +103,7 @@ function write(operator: RequestFilterOperator, value?: string | number): void {
   // which `onInput` drops as "not dirty".
   handleChange(
     control.value.path,
-    assign({}, control.value.data, { [operator]: isUnset ? null : value })
+    assign({}, { [operator]: isUnset ? null : value })
   );
 }
